# Local deny-list  —  copy to .identity/patterns.local (which is gitignored).
#
# This file exists because the strings you most need to keep out of the repo
# are your real name, your real address and your machine names, and a file
# listing them cannot itself be committed. patterns.local is in .gitignore.
# This .example copy is the committed template and must stay generic.
#
# Format, one rule per line:
#
#     SEVERITY   <regex>   ## message shown when it matches
#
# SEVERITY is BLOCK (refuses the commit) or WARN (prints, commits anyway).
# The regex is Python syntax, matched case-insensitively against every line of
# every staged text file, and against file paths.
#
# Tune it. A deny-list that fires on every commit gets bypassed with
# --no-verify within a week, and then it is protecting nothing.

BLOCK   your\.real\.address@example\.com          ## real email address
BLOCK   \bYour Real Name\b                        ## legal name
BLOCK   /home/yourrealuser\b                      ## home directory path
BLOCK   [\w.-]+@yourlaptop\b                      ## shell prompt shows the host
BLOCK   \bAcmeCorp\b                              ## employer name

# A bare hostname is usually too broad to BLOCK — if your machine is called
# "fuzzy" you will trip on every post that says "fuzzy hashing". Prefer the
# prompt-shaped form above, or downgrade the bare word to WARN:
# WARN    \byourlaptop\b                          ## possible hostname mention
